'''anatto''' (a-nat′ō), ''n.'' Same as ''arnotto''.

'''Anaxagorean''' (an-aks-ag-ọ̄-rē′a̤n), ''a.'' and ''n.'' [< L. ''Anaxagoras'', Gr. Ἀναξαγόρας.] '''I.''' ''a.'' Relating or pertaining to the person or the doctrines of Anaxagoras, a celebrated Greek philosopher, born at Clazomenæ, near Smyrna, about 500 {{sc|b. c.}} {{smaller|Anaxagoras taught the eternity of matter, and ascribed the origin of the world and the order}}

